    "summary": "An estimated 100,000 Somalis now speak Turkish, a number that continues to grow due to expanding Turkish-run schools and business ties. This trend raises questions about the future role of Arabic as the country's primary second language.",
    "content": "In Mogadishu, the Turkish language is becoming increasingly prevalent. Language educators estimate that around 100,000 Somalis have acquired proficiency in Turkish, a figure that is steadily climbing. This growth is largely attributed to the expansion of Turkish educational institutions across the capital, which offer instruction in the language.\n\nThe surge in Turkish language learning is also fueled by deepening economic ties between Somalia and Turkey. Turkish companies are active in various sectors, from construction to telecommunications, creating a demand for Somali workers who can communicate in Turkish. This practical incentive is encouraging more Somalis to enroll in language courses, both at schools and through private tutoring.\n\nObservers note that the rise of Turkish may have implications for the linguistic landscape of Somalia. Arabic has long served as the country's de facto second language, used in religious, educational, and formal contexts. The growing presence of Turkish, however, is prompting discussions about whether it could eventually rival Arabic in importance.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this linguistic shift is significant. Turkish language skills could become an asset for those seeking opportunities in Turkey, whether in business, education, or employment. Additionally, the strengthening of Turkish-Somali relations may open new connections for diaspora communities, facilitating engagement with both countries.\n\nAs Turkish language programs continue to expand and commercial ties grow, the trajectory of this trend remains to be seen. While Arabic retains its cultural and religious significance, the practical advantages of Turkish are making it an increasingly attractive option for many Somalis.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/25/turkish-language-somalia-second-language/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=turkish-language-somalia-second-language",
